- set up pipeline to extract data

  -- sample / selection bias (the score is mean of statements the LLM CHOSE to
     extract, not a representative sample) --
- we evaluate SCORING (pre-isolated statements vs gold) but never SELECTION (which
  statements get pulled). Build a selection eval: hand-label all attribute-relevant
  statements in a few articles, measure extractor recall/precision.
- missing-middle/salience bias: LLM extracts notable statements, so mean(extracted)
  != mean(all said). Score-all-then-sample to estimate the skew.
- source-mix confound: press releases (curated) vs Hansard (adversarial) vs RNZ
  (newsworthy) systematically shift scores -> cross-politician comparison partly
  measures WHERE we have data. Report/normalise by source mix.
- separate frequency from severity (current mean-of-flagged conflates them); needs
  the full denominator (rate x severity).
- surface per-attribute n + confidence band; discount/flag thin (n=1) scores.

  -- attribute-specific methodologies (food for thought, not implementing now) --
- promises/predictions/factual claims (Strength, Divination, Veracity) can't be
  scored from text alone -> build a longitudinal CLAIMS LEDGER instead. Extraction
  shifts from "score" to "extract + structure": testable proposition, type, date
  made, falsification condition, expected resolution date. A separate verification
  pass (human review now, automated later) resolves each true/false/partial with
  evidence. Score = fraction of RESOLVED claims that held true (real ground truth,
  not LLM guess). Also defuses the "calling X a liar" legal worry (evidenced
  verdict, not assertion). Seeds already exist: integrity.jsonl (contains_claim),
  evasion.jsonl, promises.txt.
- authenticity = consistency between RHETORIC and RECORD, not a per-statement score.
  Build two corpora per politician (public rhetoric vs parliamentary voting/division
  records + Hansard stances) and detect contradictions on the same topic. Needs
  Parliament division/voting data linked by topic on top of Hansard text.

